.TH SPIT 1 "October 2026" "stutools 2.0.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
spit \- Stu's powerful I/O tester, with fixed memory pools
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B spit
.BI \-f " TARGET"
.BI \-c " WORKLOAD"
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
spit runs storage workloads across workers and reports completed operations,
MiB/s, IOPS, elapsed seconds and verification counts. Linux supports io_uring,
libaio, synchronous direct and buffered I/O. macOS and Windows currently use
buffered positional I/O. The Rust implementation uses Tokio to coordinate
long-lived workers and allocates its reusable I/O pools before measurement.
.PP
Write workloads overwrite the selected range. Begin with a disposable file.
A read workload does not create a missing file or enlarge an existing file.
All ranges and pool budgets are validated before target creation or writes.
.SH EXAMPLES
Create a disposable 16 MiB file, write it once and verify every write:
.PP
.nf
  spit -f scratch.img -G 16MiB -c ws1k4q32j4x1 -v
.fi
.PP
Read it randomly for five seconds:
.PP
.nf
  spit -f scratch.img -c rs0k4q32j4 -t 5
.fi
.PP
Run a random mixed workload with Linux libaio:
.PP
.nf
  spit -f scratch.img -c rws0k4q32j8 -t 10 --backend libaio
.fi
.PP
Only overwrite the middle 512 KiB of an existing file:
.PP
.nf
  spit -f scratch.img -G 256KiB-768KiB -c ws1k4q8x1 -v
.fi
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-f " FILE"
Target file or Linux block device. Repeat for several targets. Existing size is
the default range. New or unsized targets require an explicit size.
.TP
.BI \-F " PREFIX"
One target per expanded worker, PREFIX.0001, PREFIX.0002, and so on.
.TP
.BI \-G " SIZE or LOW-HIGH"
Select [0,SIZE) or [LOW,HIGH), with the upper bound excluded. Bare numbers mean
decimal GB; explicit B, KB/MB/GB and KiB/MiB/GiB suffixes are supported.
For example, 1GiB is 1073741824 bytes. Workload G tokens instead use GiB.
.TP
.BI \-b " BYTES"
Select a size in bytes; equivalent to -G with a byte suffix.
.TP
.BI \-c " WORKLOAD"
Compact workload string. Repeat to run workloads together. Quoted strings may
contain whitespace, for example "w s1 k4 q32 j4 x1".
.TP
.BI \-t " SECONDS"
Global time limit, default 10. Use -1 for unlimited. Workload x/X pass limits
override it; Y caps operations. Workload T sets a per-worker duration.
.TP
.B \-v
Immediately read back ordinary writes and check their seeded, address-tagged
payload. Verification reads do not count toward workload read operations.
Scheduled metadata/unique checks are described below.
.TP
.BI \-R " SEED"
Random seed, default 1. Repeating it reproduces Rust's position generation;
it does not reproduce C's random sequence or C's data format.
.TP
.BI \-S " FILE"
Load positions: byte-offset, R or W, byte-length, one record per line. Example:
.nf
  0 W 4096
  4096 R 4096
.fi
These simple records are not C position files. Loaded requests must fit the
selected range and direct-I/O alignment. Every worker replays the imported range.
.TP
.BI \-p " WORKLOAD"
Precondition before the measured job. One worker only; defaults are ws1x1
with the supplied tokens appended. The last -p wins. The preconditioning
implementation does not reproduce all advanced C preconditioning modes.
.TP
.BI \-K " READS,WRITES"
Require exact completed workload counts across all workers and targets.
Verification-only reads and preconditioning are excluded.
.TP
.BI \-E " ERRORS"
Libaio submission-error stop threshold, default 1. Zero permits retries, still
subject to the hard retry limit and timeout. Any submission error makes the
final exit nonzero, even if later I/O succeeds. Completion errors stop the worker.
.TP
.BI \-T " SECONDS"
Asynchronous completion-stall timeout, default 1; accepted range (0,3600].
This differs from workload T. Synchronous calls and kernel queue destruction
cannot guarantee a bounded wait.
.TP
.BI \-\-backend " NAME"
auto, io_uring, libaio, direct or buffered; bufferedio aliases buffered. Linux
auto probes actual io_uring queue creation, falling back to libaio if unavailable.
Other systems default to buffered. Explicit selections never switch backends.
.TP
.BI \-\-memory " SIZE"
Budget for user I/O buffers and request/position tables, default 512MiB.
Thread stacks, Tokio, parsed arguments, kernel queues and page cache are extra.
This is not a process RSS limit. Startup/error/shutdown may allocate; successful
worker I/O loops report hot_path_allocations=0.
.TP
.B \-\-recreate
Allow enlargement of existing writable regular files to fit the selected range.
It does not make a read job writable or truncate an existing file's tail.
.TP
.B \-V
Accepted for C CLI compatibility; currently has no effect.
.TP
.B \-h, \-\-help
Show examples, syntax and the detected default backend.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Show the version and implementation.
.SH WORKLOAD LANGUAGE
Defaults are a 50:50 read/write mix, sequential order, 4 KiB blocks, queue depth
64 and one worker. Specify r or w explicitly when that is what you intend.
.TP
.B r, w, rw, rrw, pPROB
Read, write or mix letters proportionally; rrw is two reads per write on average.
p0.7 selects 70% reads. PROB must lie between zero and one.
.TP
.B s0, s1, s-1, sN
Random, sequential, reverse, or N interleaved stripes. Fractional magnitudes
between zero and one control partial shuffling; negative values reverse order.
.TP
.B kN, MN, kMIN-MAX, kMIN:MAX
Block sizes in KiB or MiB. kMIN-MAX chooses multiples of MIN; kMIN:MAX chooses
powers of two starting at MIN. Direct requests require sector alignment.
.TP
.B qN, qMIN-MAX
Queue depth, or maximum submission group MIN and queue depth MAX. qN permits
batching up to N. Async queue slots are reused only after completion; synchronous
backends execute one I/O at a time and use q only for reusable batch storage.
.TP
.B jN, JN
N workers with split ranges, or N workers sharing the range. Each worker has its
own queue and buffers. Total expanded workers are limited to 1024.
.TP
.B Pn, P-n, P+n, P.n
Use the first n generated positions, n random samples with replacement, n spaced
positions, or alternating low/high spaced positions. Position tables are fixed
at startup and reused; memory limits may constrain implicit table capacity.
.TP
.B Xn, xn, Yn, O
n position-table passes, n worker-range worth of bytes, n workload operations,
or one position-table pass. X and x cannot be combined with each other or with
workload T. Readbacks scheduled by metadata modes are workload operations;
final checks pending at a stopping limit are verification-only operations.
.TP
.B =, n, N, z, F
Alternate read/write actions each pass, shuffle positions each pass, shift by a
minimum block per pass, start at zero, or flush completed write batches.
.TP
.B SIOPS, TSECONDS, WON:OFF
Per-worker rate, duration and duty cycle. W1:0.5 alternates one active second
with half an idle second. Global -T is a stall timeout, not this duration.
.TP
.B GLOW-HIGH, bLOW-HIGH, G, G_
Per-workload GiB range, byte range, full target range, or target range with
worker splitting. An underscore may separate range endpoints and requests
splitting for J workers. All ranges must lie within the global target range.
.SH VERIFICATION MODES
m and mCOUNT schedule a read phase after the primary positions and flush writes;
COUNT caps queue depth. u assigns per-position 16-bit seeds, wrapping modulo
65536. U also schedules checked read pairs at queue depth one. Original read
positions are replayed without expecting newly written data.
.PP
Checks pending when a time, byte or operation limit is reached finish before
success. These final readbacks are excluded from workload counters and timed
workload seconds. verified_ops includes completed data checks. Metadata checks
cannot be combined with alternating-action =. For ordinary -v workloads,
verification occurs inside the timed loop, unlike C's final verification pass.
.SH OUTPUT AND EXIT STATUS
The summary reports read_ops, write_ops, seconds, read_MiB_s, write_MiB_s, IOPS,
hot_path_allocations and verified_ops. Throughput uses completed workload bytes;
MiB is 1048576 bytes. Too few samples produce an unavailable diagnostic rather
than a fabricated latency statistic.
.PP
Zero means success. Invalid arguments, pool/range failures, incomplete I/O,
submission errors, verification failures, allocation-audit failures and count
mismatches return nonzero. A worker setup failure aborts all workers before I/O.
.SH PERFORMANCE
Compare the same sizes, ranges, seeds, durations and backends. Buffered results
include OS page cache and are not physical-media throughput. Short RAM-device
measurements do not predict SSD/HDD performance. Mixed io_uring workloads on the
development host currently use more CPU than libaio; choose explicitly when
comparing. No general performance claim against fio is implied.
.SH COMPATIBILITY
Rust 2.0 implements the common C syntax, not every C workflow. Its data and
position formats differ from C spit and spitchecker. NUMA policies, copy/trim,
coverage/GC, changing rate schedules, position output, logs and latency histograms
remain C features. Unsupported tokens fail explicitly. Use spitc for those
workflows and consult the installed README before replacing scripts.
